\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Algorithm-confusion attack and the pinned-verifier defense. (a) A naive verifier trusts the \texttt  {alg} field of the incoming token; an attacker who can intercept the token rewrites \texttt  {alg=HS256} to \texttt  {alg=none}, strips the signature, and the verifier accepts an unauthenticated message. (b) The pinned verifier records the issuance algorithm out-of-band, then re-checks each token against the pinned algorithm rather than the token's self-described one. The ProVerif model encodes both verifiers and shows the naive path produces a counter-trace witness for the rewrite, while the pinned path admits no such trace. Runtime conformance: 5 attack patterns + 2 controls, all pass.}}{6}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:anchor-alg-confusion}{{3}{6}{Algorithm-confusion attack and the pinned-verifier defense. (a) A naive verifier trusts the \texttt {alg} field of the incoming token; an attacker who can intercept the token rewrites \texttt {alg=HS256} to \texttt {alg=none}, strips the signature, and the verifier accepts an unauthenticated message. (b) The pinned verifier records the issuance algorithm out-of-band, then re-checks each token against the pinned algorithm rather than the token's self-described one. The ProVerif model encodes both verifiers and shows the naive path produces a counter-trace witness for the rewrite, while the pinned path admits no such trace. Runtime conformance: 5 attack patterns + 2 controls, all pass}{figure.caption.4}{}}
